"Princeton University Press – Mathematics 2018 Catalog (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/math18.pdf)\nciteturn829search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Diaconis/SkyrmsTen Great Ideas about Chance ... and mathematicians transformed the idea of chance ... 2017. 272 pages. 25 halftones. 8 tables. 19 line illus. ... General Interest  1 • Textbooks & Reference  5 • Princeton Lifesaver Study Guides  9 •\n“[A] welcome contribution to the slim body of recent \nworks that popularize chance. It invites readers to \nthe examples with ease, and it raises far-reaching, \nthought-provoking questions.”\n—Joseph Mazur, author of Fluke\nDiaconis/Skyrms\nTen Great Ideas about Chance\nIn the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, gamblers \nand mathematicians transformed the idea of chance \nfrom a mystery into the discipline of probability, \nsetting the stage for a series of breakthroughs that \nenabled or transformed innumerable fi elds, from \ngambling, mathematics, statistics, economics, and \nfi nance to physics and computer science. Th is book \ntells the story of ten great ideas about chance and \nthe thinkers who developed them.\n2017. 272 pages. 25 halftones. 8 tables. 19 line illus.\nCl: 978-0-691-17416-7 \n$27.95 | £22.95\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with \nGerolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, \nmathematician, and professional gambler who \nhelped develop the idea that chance actually can be \nmeasured. Th ey describe how later thinkers showed \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTen Great Ideas About Chance | Mathematical Association of America (https://old.maa.org/press/maa-reviews/ten-great-ideas-about-chance)\nciteturn829search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Ten Great Ideas About Chance ... Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms ... Princeton University Press ... 2017 ... 272 ... There are ten chapters, each one centered around a “great idea” in the history of probability and statistics.\n\n# Ten Great Ideas About Chance\n\nImage\n\n###### \n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms\n\nPublisher: \n\nPrinceton University Press\n\nPublication Date: \n\n2017\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n272\n\nFormat: \n\nHardcover\n\nPrice: \n\n27.95\n\nISBN: \n\n9780691174167\n\nCategory: \n\nGeneral\n\nBLL Rating: \n\nBLL*\n\nThe Basic Library List Committee recommends this book for acquisition by undergraduate mathematics libraries.\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nMark Hunacek\n\n, on\n\n11/9/2017\n\nThis book discusses the mathematical, historical, philosophical, and even psychological aspects of probability and statistics. It’s a very nice book: mathematically rigorous, yet also reasonably accessible; informative, yet fun and entertaining to read. Both students and faculty should find reading this to be a rewarding experience.\n\nThe text has its origins in an interdisciplinary course taught at Stanford University by the two authors, one of whom (Diaconis) is a mathematician and the other a philosopher. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrinceton University Press Fall 2017 Catalog (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/F17Seasonal.pdf)\nciteturn829search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.5 years ago; Ten Great Ideas about ChancePersi Diaconis & Brian Skyrms ... ticians transformed the idea of chance from a mystery into the dis- ... Tricks (Princeton). ... 978-0-691-17416-7  Cloth  $27.95T\nTen Great Ideas about Chance\nPersi Diaconis & Brian Skyrms\nA fascinating account of the \nbreakthrough ideas that \ntransformed probability \nand statistics\nIn the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, gamblers and mathema-\nticians transformed the idea of chance from a mystery into the dis-\ncipline of probability, setting the stage for a series of breakthroughs \nthat enabled or transformed innumerable fields, from gambling, \nmathematics, statistics, economics, and finance to physics and \ncomputer science. This book tells the story of ten great ideas about \nchance and the thinkers who developed them, tracing the philosoph-\nical implications of these ideas as well as their mathematical impact.\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Girolamo Cardano, \na sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gam-\nbler who helped develop the idea that chance can actually be mea-\nsured. They describe how later thinkers showed how the judgment \nof chance can also be measured, how frequency is related to chance, \nand how chance, judgment, and frequency could be unified. Diaconis \nand Skyrms explain how Thomas Bayes laid the foundation of mod-\nern statistics, and they explore David Hume’s problem of induction, \nAndrey Kolmogorov’s general mathematical framework for proba-\nbility, the application of computability to chance, and why chance is \nessential to modern physics. A final idea—that we are psychologically \npredisposed to error when judging chance—is taken up through the \nwork of Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.\nComplete with a brief probability refresher, Ten Great Ideas \nabout Chance is certain to be a hit with anyone who wants to under-\nstand the secrets of probability and how they were discovered.\n“This compelling book is a welcome \ncontribution to the slim body of \nrecent works that popularize chance. \nIt invites readers to the examples \nwith ease, and it raises far-reaching, \nthought-provoking questions. \nI learned a huge amount of useful \nnew information about probability \ntheory and statistics from this book.”\n—Joseph Mazur, author of Fluke: \nThe Math and Myth of Coincidence\nPersi Diaconis is the Mary V. Sunseri Professor of Statistics and \nMathematics at Stanford University and the coauthor of Magical \nMathematics: The Mathematical Ideas That Animate Great Magic \nTricks (Princeton). Brian Skyrms is Distinguished Professor in the \nDepartment of Logic and Philosophy at the University of California, \nIrvine, and Professor of Philosophy at Stanford University. His books \ninclude From Zeno to Arbitrage.\nDECEMBER\n978-0-691-17416-7  Cloth  $27.95T\n280 pages. 25 halftones. 19 line illus. 8 tables. 6 x 9.\nPOPULAR MATHEMATICS\n10  Trade--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTen great ideas about chance by Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms, pp. 272, £22.95 (hard), ISBN 978-0-69117-416-7, Princeton University Press (2017). | The Mathematical Gazette | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/mathematical-gazette/article/ten-great-ideas-about-chance-by-persi-diaconis-and-brian-skyrms-pp-272-2295-hard-isbn-9780691174167-princeton-university-press-2017/E765081A5A754CAF1F7243405CE08E2F)\nciteturn829search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Ten great ideas about chance by Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms, pp.272, £22.95 (hard), ISBN 978-0-69117-416-7, Princeton University Press (2017).\n\n# Ten great ideas about chance by Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms, pp. 272, £22.95 (hard), ISBN 978-0-69117-416-7, Princeton University Press (2017).\n\n## Review products\n\nTen great ideas about chance by Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms, pp. 272, £22.95 (hard), ISBN 978-0-69117-416-7, Princeton University Press (2017).\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  17 October 2018\n\nOwen Toller\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nOwen Toller*\n    Affiliation:\n\nSt Paul's School, Lonsdale Road, London SW13 9JT e-mail: olct@stpaulsschool.org.uk\n\n* * *\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn abstract is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.\n\nImage: Image of the first page of this content. For PDF version, please use the ‘Save PDF’ preceeding this image.'\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Reviews\n\nInformation\n    \n\nThe Mathematical Gazette , Volume 102 , Issue 555 , November 2018 , pp. 567 - 568\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/mag.2018.155 [Opens in a new window]\n\n## References\n\n1\n\n1. Marrero, Osvaldo, What is Bayesian statistics?, Math. Gaz. 100 (July 2016), pp. 247–256.Google Scholar\n\n\nTen great ideas about chance by Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms, pp. 272, £22.95 (hard), ISBN 978-0-69117-416-7, Princeton University Press (2017).\n\n  * Volume 102, Issue 555\n  * Owen Toller ^{(a1)}\n  * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/mag.2018.155\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTen Great Ideas about Chance | JSTOR (https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.14)\nciteturn829search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Copyright Date: 2018https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33Published by: Princeton University Press ... Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Gerolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance actually can be measured. ... Diaconis and Skyrms explain how Thomas Bayes laid the foundation of modern statistics, and they explore David Hume's problem of induction, Andrey Kolmogorov's general mathematical framework for probability, the application of computability to chance, and why chance is essential to modern physics. ... Complete with a brief probability refresher, Ten Great Ideas about Chance is certain to be a hit with anyone who wants to understand the secrets of probability and how they were discovered.\n\nTen Great Ideas about Chance\n\nPERSI DIACONIS\n\nBRIAN SKYRMS\n\nCopyright Date: 2018\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33\n\nPublished by: Princeton University Press\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33\n\nBook Info\n\nDescription\n\nA fascinating account of the breakthrough ideas that transformed probability and statistics\n\nIn the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, gamblers and mathematicians transformed the idea of chance from a mystery into the discipline of probability, setting the stage for a series of breakthroughs that enabled or transformed innumerable fields, from gambling, mathematics, statistics, economics, and finance to physics and computer science. This book tells the story of ten great ideas about chance and the thinkers who developed them, tracing the philosophical implications of these ideas as well as their mathematical impact.\n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Gerolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance actually can be measured.   2. Table of Contents\n\nTable of Contents (pp. vii-viii)\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.2\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33.2\n\n  3. PREFACE\n\nPREFACE (pp. ix-x)\n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.3\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33.3\n\n  4. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\nACKNOWLEDGMENTS (pp. xi-xiv)\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.4\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33.4\n\n  5. MEASUREMENT\n\nCHAPTER 1 MEASUREMENT (pp. 1-21)\n\nGerolamo Cardano\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.5\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33.5\n\nOne way to understand the roots of a subject is to examine how its originators thought about it. Some basic philosophical issues are already evident at the very beginning. The first great idea is simply that chance can be measured. It emerged during the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, and it is something of a mystery why it took so long. The Greeks had a goddess of chance, Tyche. Democritus and his followers postulated a physical chance affecting all the atoms that made up the universe. This is the “swerve” of atoms in Lucretius’ De Rerum Natura. Games of chance, using...\n\n  6. JUDGMENT\n\nCHAPTER 2 JUDGMENT (pp. 22-47)\n\nFrank Plumpton Ramsey\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33.6\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33.6\n\nOur second great idea is that judgments can be measured and that coherent judgments are probabilities. (What exactly is meant by coherence will be made clear.) In the classic gambling games of chapter 1, our judgments were informed by symmetry. Symmetrical cases were judged equiprobable. In this chapter we will see how degrees of belief implicit in judgments about all sorts of cases can also be measured. When they are so measured, coherent judgments turn out to have the same mathematical structure as that discovered by Cardano and Galileo by counting equiprobable outcomes in gambling.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTen Great Ideas about Chance | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33)\nciteturn829search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Copyright Date: 2018https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33Published by: Princeton University Press ... Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Gerolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance actually can be measured. ... Diaconis and Skyrms explain how Thomas Bayes laid the foundation of modern statistics, and they explore David Hume's problem of induction, Andrey Kolmogorov's general mathematical framework for probability, the application of computability to chance, and why chance is essential to modern physics. ... Complete with a brief probability refresher, Ten Great Ideas about Chance is certain to be a hit with anyone who wants to understand the secrets of probability and how they were discovered.\n\nImage: Cover of Ten Great Ideas about Chance\n\nTen Great Ideas about Chance\n\nPERSI DIACONIS\n\nBRIAN SKYRMS\n\nCopyright Date: 2018\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvc77m33\n\nPublished by: Princeton University Press\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvc77m33\n\nSearch for reviews of this book\n\nCite\n\nBook Info\n\nDescription\n\nA fascinating account of the breakthrough ideas that transformed probability and statistics\n\nIn the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, gamblers and mathematicians transformed the idea of chance from a mystery into the discipline of probability, setting the stage for a series of breakthroughs that enabled or transformed innumerable fields, from gambling, mathematics, statistics, economics, and finance to physics and computer science. This book tells the story of ten great ideas about chance and the thinkers who developed them, tracing the philosophical implications of these ideas as well as their mathematical impact.\n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Gerolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance actually can be measured. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying Chance: Understanding the Secrets of Probability | SIAM (https://www.siam.org/publications/siam-news/articles/demystifying-chance-understanding-the-secrets-of-probability/)\nciteturn829search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Image: <em>Ten Great Ideas about Chance.</em> By Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. ... Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, November 2017. 272 pages, $27.95. ... Diaconis and Skyrms’ second great idea is, unsurprisingly, that one can infer probabilities in situations where a priori estimates are either unavailable or unreliable.\n\nVolume 51 Issue 03 April 2018\n\nBook Reviews\n\n# Demystifying Chance: Understanding the Secrets of Probability\n\nApril 02, 2018\n\nBy\n\n  * James Case\n\nImage: <em>Ten Great Ideas about Chance.</em> By Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. Courtesy of Princeton University Press. Ten Great Ideas about Chance. By Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. Courtesy of Princeton University Press.\n\nTen Great Ideas about Chance. By Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms. Princeton University Press, Princeton, NJ, November 2017. 272 pages, $27.95.\n\nFor the better part of a decade, Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms taught a course at Stanford University on the history, philosophy, and common foundations of probability and statistics. With the passage of time, they realized that the story they were telling would likely be of interest to a larger audience. Thus, Ten Great Ideas about Chance was born.\n\nAs the title suggests, the book consists of 10 chapters exploring 10 significant ideas about chance. An appendix offers a tutorial on probability and extensive chapter notes, an index, and an “annotated select bibliography.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutumn 2019 Princeton University Press Catalog (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/F19Seasonal.pdf)\nciteturn829search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.5 years ago; PERSI DIACONIS & BRIAN SKYRMS ... Sunseri Professor of Statistics and Mathematics at Stanford University and the coauthor of *Magical Mathematics: The Mathematical Ideas That Animate Great Magic Tricks* (Princeton). ... 272 pages. 44 b/w illus. 8 tables. 5 x 8.9780691174167 Hardback 2017 ... Book cover image for *Ten Great Ideas About Chance* in blue and yellow, showing two stylized hands and the title text on the cover; to the right is an italic pull-quote: “A fascinating account of the breakthrough ideas that transformed probability and statistics”.\nIt is also thoroughly engaging.”  \n—David M. Bressoud, *The UMAP Journal*\n\n“A very enriching journey. Your vision will be broadened.”  \n—Adhemar Bultheel, European Mathematical Society\n\nPersi Diaconis is the Mary V. Sunseri Professor of Statistics and Mathematics at Stanford University and the coauthor of *Magical Mathematics: The Mathematical Ideas That Animate Great Magic Tricks* (Princeton). Brian Skyrms is Distinguished Professor in the Department of Logic and Philosophy at the University of California, Irvine, and Professor of Philosophy at Stanford University. His books include *Signals: Evolution, Learning, and Information*.\n\nOCTOBER\n\n9780691196398 Paperback $17.95T  \n272 pages. 44 b/w illus. 8 tables. 5 x 8.\n\n9780691174167 Hardback 2017  \n9781400888283 E-book\n\nPOPULAR SCIENCE | MATHEMATICS\n\nPaperbacks 91\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBook cover image for *Ten Great Ideas About Chance* in blue and yellow, showing two stylized hands and the title text on the cover; to the right is an italic pull-quote: “A fascinating account of the breakthrough ideas that transformed probability and statistics”.\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nhsk17.indd (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/hps17.pdf)\nciteturn829search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.5 years ago; Ten Great Ideasabout Chance ... 2017. 216 pages. 11 color plates. 30 halftones. ... “Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms ... press.princeton.edu\nForthcoming\nTen Great Ideas \nabout Chance\nPersi Diaconis & \nBrian Skyrms\nNew in Paperback\nT e Golden Ticket\nP, NP, and the Search for \nthe Impossible\nLance Fortnow\nNew in Paperback\nHeavenly Mathematics\nT e Forgotten Art of \nSpherical Trigonometry\nGlen Van Brummelen\n2017. 216 pages. 11 color plates. 30 halftones. \n93 line illus.\nPa: 978-0-691-17599-7 \n$19.95 | £14.95\nCl: 978-0-691-14892-2 \n$39.95 | £32.95\nOne of Choice’s Outstanding Academic \nTitles for 2013\nShortlisted for the 2013 Neumann Book \nPrize, British Society for the History of \nMathematics\n“T is wonderful book looks at \nboth the nature of probability \nand the history of thinking on \nthe subject, following a path \nthat goes from coin-ﬂ ipping \nmachines to quantum physics \nvia psychology and behavioral \neconomics. . . . Outstanding!”\n—Stephen Schaefer, London \nBusiness School\n“You will love this book. It’s \ncompletely accessible and \ncaptures the thrill, potential, \nand heartbreak of an edgy \nmathematical problem in terms \nthat nonmathematicians will \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrinceton University Press Fall 2017 Catalog - Trade (https://assets.press.princeton.edu/catalogs/F17Trade.pdf)\nciteturn829search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.5 years ago; Crawled: 9.5 years ago; Book cover image for “TEN GREAT IDEAS ABOUT CHANCE” by Persi Diaconis & Brian Skyrms, shown at upper left on a dark blue/purple cover with large stacked light-blue title text and a few coin-like objects over the cover. ... Persi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Girolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance can actually be measured. ... Sunseri Professor of Statistics and Mathematics at Stanford University and the coauthor of *Magical Mathematics: The Mathematical Ideas That Animate Great Magic Tricks* (Princeton).\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBook cover image for “TEN GREAT IDEAS ABOUT CHANCE” by Persi Diaconis & Brian Skyrms, shown at upper left on a dark blue/purple cover with large stacked light-blue title text and a few coin-like objects over the cover.\n</visual_element>\n\nTen Great Ideas about Chance  \nPERSI DIACONIS & BRIAN SKYRMS\n\nIn the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, gamblers and mathematicians transformed the idea of chance from a mystery into the discipline of probability, setting the stage for a series of breakthroughs that enabled or transformed innumerable fields, from gambling, mathematics, statistics, economics, and finance to physics and computer science. This book tells the story of ten great ideas about chance and the thinkers who developed them, tracing the philosophical implications of these ideas as well as their mathematical impact.\n\nPersi Diaconis and Brian Skyrms begin with Girolamo Cardano, a sixteenth-century physician, mathematician, and professional gambler who helped develop the idea that chance can actually be measured. They describe how later thinkers showed how the judgment of chance can also be measured, how frequency is related to chance, and how chance, judgment, and frequency could be unified. Diaconis and Skyrms explain how Thomas Bayes laid the foundation of modern statistics, and they explore David Hume’s problem of induction, Andrey Kolmogorov’s general mathematical framework for probability, the application of computability to chance, and why chance is essential to modern physics. A final idea—that we are psychologically predisposed to error when judging chance—is taken up through the work of Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.\n\nComplete with a brief probability refresher, *Ten Great Ideas about Chance* is certain to be a hit with anyone who wants to understand the secrets of probability and how they were discovered.\n\nPersi Diaconis is the Mary V. Sunseri Professor of Statistics and Mathematics at Stanford University and the coauthor of *Magical Mathematics: The Mathematical Ideas That Animate Great Magic Tricks* (Princeton). Brian Skyrms is Distinguished Professor in the Department of Logic and Philosophy at the University of California, Irvine, and Professor of Philosophy at Stanford University. His books include *From Zeno to Arbitrage*.\n\nDECEMBER  \n978-0-691-17416-7  Cloth  $27.95T  \n280 pages. 25 halftones. 19 line illus. 8 tables. 6 x 9.\n\nPOPULAR MATHEMATICS\n\nA fascinating account of the breakthrough ideas that transformed probability and statistics\n\n“This compelling book is a welcome contribution to the slim body of recent works that popularize chance. It invites readers to the examples with ease, and it raises far-reaching, thought-provoking questions. I learned a huge amount of useful new information about probability theory and statistics from this book.”  \n—Joseph Mazur, author of *Fluke: The Math and Myth of Coincidence*\n\n10 Trade--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews A. Bultheel (https://people.cs.kuleuven.be/~adhemar.bultheel/WWW/EMS/r233.php)\nciteturn829search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Author(s): Persi Diaconis, Brian Skyrmstitle: Ten Great Ideas about ChancePublisher: Princeton University PressYear: 2017 ... Pages: 272 pp. ... Kolmogorov has defined probability, but the idea of chance has evolved in several stages before this definition emerged.\nAuthor(s): Persi Diaconis, Brian Skyrms\ntitle: Ten Great Ideas about Chance\nPublisher: Princeton University Press\nYear: 2017\nISBN: 9780691174167 (hbk)\nPages: 272 pp.\nPrice (tentative): £ 22.95 (hbk) (net)\nURL: press.princeton.edu/titles/11082.html\nShort description:\nThe authors start from the early days of probability applied in gambling and work their way through ten great ideas that have shaped the current day scenery of probability and statistics. The discussion is fundamental and covers the different philosophical interpretations of concepts such as frequency versus mathematical probability, Bayesian probability, subjective probability, entropy, quantum physics, and inductive reasoning. For the reader who needs to refresh notions from elementary probability a short tutorial is provided.\nMSC main category: 60\nMSC category: 60-03\nOther MSC categories: 60A05, 62-03, 62A01  |  | \nReview:\n\nThis book grew out of a course given by the authors, a statistician (Diaconis) and a philosopher (Skyrms) to a mixed audience of students. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVol. 32, No. 1, 2019 (https://chance.amstat.org/files/2019/02/CHANCE-32.1.pdf)\nciteturn829search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; 10 Great Ideas ... Persi Diaconis and Brian SkyrmsHardcover: 272 pages ... Year: 2018 ... Publisher: Princeton ... ISBN-13: 978-0691174167 ... about the idea that “chance is frequency.”\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTen Great Ideas about Chance - Persi Diaconis, Frederick Brian Skyrms - Google Libros (https://books.google.com.mx/books?id=gkE4DwAAQBAJ)\nciteturn829se